# CruiseShipDB — Schema and Relationship Notes

## Purpose
Educational Microsoft SQL Server database modeling a cruise operation: departure ports, voyages, voyage cabins, passengers, crew, and bookable activities.

## Tables and keys
| Table | Primary key | Foreign keys | Meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| Ports | PortID | — | Departure ports and docking fees |
| Voyages | VoyageID | DeparturePortID → Ports.PortID | Trips and schedules |
| Cabins | CabinID | VoyageID → Voyages.VoyageID | Cabins linked to voyages |
| Passengers | PassengerID | CabinID → Cabins.CabinID | Passenger identity and cabin assignment |
| Crew | CrewID | VoyageID → Voyages.VoyageID | Crew assignments |
| Activities | ActivityID | — | Available activities |
| Passenger_Activities | BookingID | PassengerID → Passengers.PassengerID; ActivityID → Activities.ActivityID | Passenger activity bookings |

## Cardinalities
- **Ports (1) → Voyages (many):** a port can be referenced by several voyages; each voyage has one departure port.
- **Voyages (1) → Cabins (many):** a voyage can have several cabins; each cabin row belongs to one voyage.
- **Cabins (1) → Passengers (many):** a cabin can be referenced by several passengers; each passenger row references one cabin.
- **Voyages (1) → Crew (many):** a voyage can have multiple crew rows.
- **Passengers (many) ↔ Activities (many):** implemented as two 1:M relationships through `Passenger_Activities`.

